#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383160 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce66abc2-1493-50b5-a986-b312bd69b0d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383162} {:answer/id #uuid "97a0676c-47bb-533c-80d9-c0d75ef46077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(-i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383163} {:answer/id #uuid "83aa1130-305c-5cb1-ac5b-814ca52426a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383164} {:answer/id #uuid "21c379b6-9115-5238-9915-dbace494a5e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383165} {:answer/id #uuid "e6bd01d5-5f84-5141-bac1-7784075d4ae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383166}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383164} :answer-field/id #uuid "20194f88-b71b-5ea3-a75b-4873d6a1dd62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d9318eb5-4446-512c-876e-9868eec1bc8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-130274" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(2,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(2,-2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-2).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(2,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right))j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{1+{\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}{1+{\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-2) & =-\\frac{(-2)}{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2}{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{8}\\,i+\\frac{2}{8}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(2,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(2,-2)}{‖∇f(2,-2)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{4}(i+j)}{‖\\frac{1}{4}(i+j)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed39b029-85c3-5e97-8954-e0c143d2660e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\,j+4\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383225} {:answer/id #uuid "09fedb26-ce0e-5168-aa47-838277d2a8de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383226} {:answer/id #uuid "f08ae5ee-40c5-51a6-9971-2dada660033f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,i-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383227} {:answer/id #uuid "0e4bca3e-62b0-506e-ab11-64664053f91f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\,i-2\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383228} {:answer/id #uuid "ec84b4f7-88ba-52da-a5bb-02c8fc23e908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\,i+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383229}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383229} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d2c4aa2-5988-5483-be94-986ea095a0e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "14180128-1a3b-5df5-945e-e4a1d12a6375" :question/math-academy-id "q-131117" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{3}yz-xy{z}^{2}$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-2,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}yz-xy{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}yz-xy{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{3}yz-xy{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}yz-y{z}^{2})\\text{ }i+({x}^{3}z-x{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+({x}^{3}y-2xyz)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-2,1) & =(3(1)^{2}(-2)(1)-(-2)(1)^{2})\\,i+((1)^{3}(1)-(1)(1)^{2})\\,j+((1)^{3}(-2)-2(1)(-2)(1))\\,k \\\\  & =-4\\,i+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-4\\,i+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "026c9515-f47f-5873-a2ad-edb3df11eb6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨2,0,8⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383232} {:answer/id #uuid "701877fc-bf3b-56db-a45f-7c6a12a05e62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨2,0,4⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383233} {:answer/id #uuid "b1dc8491-4f00-53bb-a2f3-1cc30657fc1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨4,-1,8⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383234} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb7af74-9d1c-58ad-9346-e5346aec63d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2,0,8⟩+t\\,⟨-1,3,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383235} {:answer/id #uuid "bf422347-5e08-54dc-812b-c19e297a836d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2,0,8⟩+t\\,⟨2,0,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383236}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383232} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5bb21b4-51b2-5e7e-bdf4-252b406432c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2fb8c052-74b8-5a3f-8748-9c302788bd84" :question/math-academy-id "q-131124" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface $4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2}={x}^{2}-6x+12$ at the point $(2,0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}-6x \\\\ 4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+6x & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+6x$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=12.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(2,0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,0,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+6x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+6x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+6x)k \\\\  & =(6-2x)\\,i-8y\\,j+8z\\,k \\\\ ∇f(2,0,1) & =(6-2(2))\\,i-8(0)\\text{ }j+8(1)\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+8\\,k \\\\  & =⟨2,0,8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(2,0,1)$ and passes through the point $(2,0,1)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,∇f(2,0,1)=⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨2,0,8⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383237 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "356b8022-b7d2-533d-94f4-726922eda15a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-x=\\frac{y+1}{5}=\\frac{z+1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383239} {:answer/id #uuid "e09b8534-738c-5763-a9b2-970a08b191e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-2=y+1=\\frac{z+1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383240} {:answer/id #uuid "ef55da50-663f-5be8-a53f-b873d7c761d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{2}=y+1=\\frac{z+1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383241} {:answer/id #uuid "8e5e3e97-6073-5e4d-bc6c-d357a63433cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2-x}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{3}=z+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383242} {:answer/id #uuid "ec59022a-f4b9-5231-92ea-f9fef2e55eb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-x=y+1=z+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383243}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383239} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ee6c88e-5b3b-502a-94c3-437af9351333" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d47e612d-20bf-521c-bc95-454c7d00a756" :question/math-academy-id "q-131129" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface ${e}^{x+y+z}-2xyz=-3$ at the point $(2,-1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)={e}^{x+y+z}-2xyz.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-3.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(2,-1,-1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1,-1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y+z}-2xyz)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y+z}-2xyz)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{x+y+z}-2xyz)k \\\\  & =({e}^{x+y+z}-2yz)\\text{ }i+({e}^{x+y+z}-2xz)\\text{ }j+({e}^{x+y+z}-2xy)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,-1,-1) & =({e}^{2-1-1}-2(-1)(-1))\\text{ }i+({e}^{2-1-1}-2(2)(-1))\\text{ }j+({e}^{2-1-1}-2(2)(-1))\\text{ }k \\\\  & =({e}^{0}-2)\\text{ }i+({e}^{0}+4)\\text{ }j+({e}^{0}+4)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =-i+5\\,j+5\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,5,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{(-1)}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{5}=\\frac{z-(-1)}{5} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{(-1)}=\\frac{y+1}{5}=\\frac{z+1}{5} \\\\ 2-x=\\frac{y+1}{5}=\\frac{z+1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383501 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c0a2d56-1032-5653-bdf7-fcbbe224983e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{u+v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383503} {:answer/id #uuid "9adbf7bb-44c3-5433-afaf-0547a0ee87dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{u}{u+v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383504} {:answer/id #uuid "39011aac-b9c0-5a20-9843-852f2d31526e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{v}{u+v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383505} {:answer/id #uuid "bab3f78e-3f56-585e-b9ce-112a1f07828c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{u+v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383506} {:answer/id #uuid "a2cf863f-082a-5c11-b7dd-3625aad8b760" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u+v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383507}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383503} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dd5cc0a-1c3d-5de1-81cf-3a2da295967f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1d4d00b9-f07a-56e5-8c62-1dc781808a7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-160404" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3u-5v \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(u+v)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=3u-5v$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u+v).$  \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-5 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{u+v} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{u+v}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{u+v} & \\frac{1}{u+v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{u+v}\\right)-(-5)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{u+v}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{u+v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383508 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "edb38347-2e82-5d17-ad40-6a0e81c54f68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{u+v}(u+v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383510} {:answer/id #uuid "838ab111-2c3c-527c-8408-c8c361dfef0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{u+v}(u+v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383511} {:answer/id #uuid "2e4c3726-e2bb-58c5-a4ce-829f4ad3a100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{u+v}(uv)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383512} {:answer/id #uuid "a7595bc5-8856-5a45-a98a-43e423b6db07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{u+v}(v-u)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383513} {:answer/id #uuid "85fd7556-d652-5f2d-a0d4-cf974f4ae8fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{u+v}(u-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383514}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383513} :answer-field/id #uuid "b727cda1-e9ad-5bca-9a2b-b11956b21803" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dd9f0081-13ed-51ab-acfe-3bfbc0b818a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-160406" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T(\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x={e}^{u+v}$ and $y={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u+v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & ={e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{u+v} & {e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\,\\text{ }2u\\, & \\,\\text{ }2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}\\cdot 2v-{e}^{u+v}\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =2{e}^{u+v}(v-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383515 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b616ca6f-9ed2-50cf-882d-c8ff1503540f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383517} {:answer/id #uuid "e8495e34-5713-5977-90f2-f1d23c192117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383518} {:answer/id #uuid "f3941799-b16e-5c96-afab-c5161cad125c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383519} {:answer/id #uuid "8235a654-792e-5e9c-921e-555040b5595a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383520} {:answer/id #uuid "2326b5cf-7dc7-542b-9226-1befd327ddd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383521}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383517} :answer-field/id #uuid "eeaaf6e0-abe7-5c25-953e-f15cf6c1b87a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383516}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b7031c6b-f8db-5a21-9b28-fac40f0a2c66" :question/math-academy-id "q-160434" :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi /4 \\\\ \\pi /4\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v).$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v))\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v))-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v))\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi /4 \\\\ \\pi /4\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is \n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383547 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d480733d-2bef-5e70-8bd1-b1ffc7f1c3fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/42/427cb4b8e6497f75aecfe809aa084b0bf6f4ad167f3a0838aa40b1a50d115e1d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383549} {:answer/id #uuid "c32a224b-bb0d-59ed-877d-57206e5e909b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d4/d477a9f312fc2fb0a5a4c2d13164c96118aa70b53cf23db80be9ec698151e6fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383550} {:answer/id #uuid "ecaf9992-c899-5cf7-95ad-9660ca75dce5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/fe/fe7bb422a3f992ea88a900e63b2c55df99bce3a785929901c248abadfadf23f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383551} {:answer/id #uuid "153ff83b-ae84-5113-a031-ffd9105e29be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/30/3033f0ae73e3f484fc5d3d65e388062feba1824fb3da9971d560cf2f518f49bb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383552} {:answer/id #uuid "c24435eb-c7ff-5866-926c-e388c2da1faf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/0b/0b764651dbc7999e7af13214685796b5045fab89f101bbc72bf413112df3c51f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383553}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383549} :answer-field/id #uuid "32f7d7e4-05aa-5348-8c27-28ad80ff2ef5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "927b4bc3-8cb1-536c-9f61-04311b6ad547" :question/math-academy-id "q-161835" :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}uv \\\\ u+v\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}uv \\\\ u+v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}v & u \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =v\\cdot 1-1\\cdot u \\\\  & =v-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v-u & >0 \\\\ v & >u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=u\n$$\n\ndefines a line, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,2)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/42/427cb4b8e6497f75aecfe809aa084b0bf6f4ad167f3a0838aa40b1a50d115e1d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383568 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f94640a-439d-5450-b89c-214d6351fa55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383570} {:answer/id #uuid "22b7af57-2308-539f-b4dc-7bf734738b88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383571} {:answer/id #uuid "305c7067-b121-52de-832b-2cd582abe378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383572} {:answer/id #uuid "0e57c966-7e8f-5c20-b14e-3fff17deba68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383573} {:answer/id #uuid "c14d8db9-1f54-501a-a0d4-dbdd47484709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383574}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383572} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fd0ed70-8ad1-5612-8701-9b382b52743e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6f0941ca-edd5-5538-8a0c-b3d25f8e41f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161877" :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(2,1)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u-v) \\\\ v-{u}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{u-v} & -\\frac{1}{u-v} \\\\ -2u & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u-v}\\cdot 1-(-2u)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{u-v}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2u}{u-v} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(2,1)} & ={\\left(\\frac{1-2u}{u-v}\\right)\\,|}_{u=2\\,v=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2(2)}{2-1} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|-3|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =3\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383575 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61164a03-d653-5509-8896-92a9f5d833f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383577} {:answer/id #uuid "5766d69f-6242-5cfa-96ee-c8cd587905ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383578} {:answer/id #uuid "6ec2c14a-52b6-5478-a60f-1f678392ec7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383579} {:answer/id #uuid "5317cbac-448e-539f-b6c8-0a7df3081b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383580} {:answer/id #uuid "18259f51-378f-592e-be8d-c8109a57fd90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383581}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383578} :answer-field/id #uuid "c852d323-c95a-5a6d-9333-8d677385a559" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3a2efb4f-04d0-536e-850b-89df0bdf4245" :question/math-academy-id "q-161878" :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(-3,3)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+{v}^{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2v \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)\\cdot 2v \\\\  & =(1-2v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-3,3)} & =(1-2v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)\\,{|}_{u=-3\\,v=3} \\\\  & =(1-2(3))\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3+3) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|-5|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =5\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385331 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94b23f6d-fcbc-5f05-bdfa-551e5a601813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{2xz}\\text{ }(z\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1)i+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}-1)\\text{ }j+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1)\\text{ }k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385333} {:answer/id #uuid "5cb1b5c1-0c20-551c-a0c4-1c105471790f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{2xz}\\text{ }(zi+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}-1)\\text{ }j+x\\text{ }k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385334} {:answer/id #uuid "0886912b-e3e5-5d97-a4f7-8d67907aca83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1)i+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}-1)\\text{ }j+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1)\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385335} {:answer/id #uuid "d8f5b6bf-05e5-54bc-b294-aa5357db39ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1)\\text{ }(zi+y\\text{ }j+x\\text{ }k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385336} {:answer/id #uuid "24c26190-fbac-5847-9741-c5fdf4a37862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{2xz}\\text{ }(zi+y\\text{ }j+x\\text{ }k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385337}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385333} :answer-field/id #uuid "63bf46dd-0351-5c7b-a783-d87b1b22bab9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7f2c8781-0baa-5cdd-8cbc-03cc6b1166f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-35989" :question/problem "Given  $g(x,y,z)={e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1)$, find $∇g(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1)) \\\\  & =2z{e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1) \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1)) \\\\  & =2y{e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}-1) \\\\ {g}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1)) \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & ={g}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }i+{g}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{g}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2z{e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1)\\text{ }i+2y{e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}-1)\\text{ }j+2x{e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2{e}^{2xz}\\text{ }(z\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1)i+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}-1)\\text{ }j+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1)\\text{ }k)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385344 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "633422e1-8e43-5c9f-91f7-5f1b7bac7900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "yz\\text{ }i+xz\\text{ }j+xy\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385346} {:answer/id #uuid "c843fcb2-eb1c-505e-b82f-631646ec1f8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+1)(yz\\text{ }i+xz\\text{ }j+xy\\text{ }k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385347} {:answer/id #uuid "021bd062-132f-5e06-92b6-5a56c9d30357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+1)(x\\text{ }i+y\\text{ }j+z\\text{ }k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385348} {:answer/id #uuid "3e3b7394-d1cf-599d-a5a4-871fc9682748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)(yz\\text{ }i+xz\\text{ }j+xy\\text{ }k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385349} {:answer/id #uuid "8fef3c82-4839-5c58-8b07-a4fabcf9cc44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+1)(\\text{ }i+\\text{ }j+\\text{ }k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385350}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385347} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d7b2133-c26f-5485-a0f6-31ac0fd776d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bcc7ffb5-272b-56a1-a959-51ef6109597c" :question/math-academy-id "q-36012" :question/problem "Given $h(x,y,z)=xyz\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)$, find  $∇h(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =yz\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+xyz\\frac{yz}{xyz} \\\\  & =yz(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+1) \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xyz\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+xyz\\frac{xz}{xyz} \\\\  & =xz(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+1) \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xyz\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+xyz\\frac{xy}{xyz} \\\\  & =xy(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇h(x,y,z) & ={h}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }i+{h}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{h}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =yz(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+1)\\text{ }i+xz(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+1)\\text{ }j+xy(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+1)(yz\\text{ }i+xz\\text{ }j+xy\\text{ }k).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385377 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a64d29b1-e2ba-5e48-b58f-cbe663d22375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{2-z}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385379} {:answer/id #uuid "12178fb2-90d2-55f5-8547-92f9528d658e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{4}=2y-2=\\frac{2-z}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385380} {:answer/id #uuid "81c5f623-6c5c-5ccc-9c48-e4f0e0bb30c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{4}=2y-2=\\frac{z-2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385381} {:answer/id #uuid "80aa2bbc-5891-5ead-81a6-a88d99d82419" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{2}=2y-2=\\frac{2-z}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385382} {:answer/id #uuid "c7afb82c-1d04-5f3a-b6b5-2604eb2833f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{4}=y-1=\\frac{2-z}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385383}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385380} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b8dbb5c-29ef-5a51-b4a1-be1ea897551a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "780bb1b8-cd5e-553c-8c69-1da3293bbe1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-37232" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z}=1$ at the point $(2,1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z}.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=1.$     \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(2,1,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,1,2).$\n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,1,2):$    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z}}\\text{ }j-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z}}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,1,2) & =\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot {2}^{2}+1-4\\cdot 2}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2\\cdot {2}^{2}+1-4\\cdot 2}}\\text{ }j-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot {2}^{2}+1-4\\cdot 2}}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =4\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }j-2\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by using the symmetric form of the equation of the line that is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$,  we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{\\frac{1}{2}}=\\frac{z-2}{-2} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{4}=2y-2=\\frac{2-z}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385408 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03936218-a08a-5085-89bd-b21159a8a2d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡v}{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385410} {:answer/id #uuid "238d09ca-3e08-5ff2-b3e4-4a29a753d669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡v}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385411} {:answer/id #uuid "71ccc347-7069-5697-97bd-d00b2c229b66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡v}{{cos}^{2}⁡u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385412} {:answer/id #uuid "972dd51b-f2ed-5cc0-a383-aec169b7a34c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡v}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right)}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385413} {:answer/id #uuid "ede2af95-8e48-5ae2-8728-186263d78888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡v}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right)}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385414}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385410} :answer-field/id #uuid "46c8b93f-fcc0-528b-abf0-be921590f6fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b88d393d-4f24-5726-a4c5-591ca47c0041" :question/math-academy-id "q-38241" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $$\n\\displaystyle x(u,v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right)\n$$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡v}{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right)} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡v}{2{cos}^{2}⁡(u/2)} & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\,0\\, & \\,\\text{ }\\text{ }\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡v}{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385501 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b5054b3-9e44-5b99-bd16-9b033843680e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\text{ }i+(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385503} {:answer/id #uuid "05942608-4f0c-5190-9795-fc199ef694f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\text{ }i+(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385504} {:answer/id #uuid "1a7a4935-ee9f-5a3b-a985-9a5dab391c99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385505} {:answer/id #uuid "ed4bed73-4b7c-549c-929b-e9d32c41ffd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\text{ }i+(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385506} {:answer/id #uuid "9fd98997-fd6e-50eb-b908-a7bc2293d619" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\text{ }i+(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385507}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385507} :answer-field/id #uuid "7eb508af-a083-5c2f-a34b-a0c8200b10d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4ef41413-e673-510f-9d54-17f57ba92ac8" :question/math-academy-id "q-71350" :question/problem "Given  $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$, find $∇f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\text{ }i+(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385508 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79312bdb-f048-5202-b77a-86cf88a3b986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{41}(5i+4j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385510} {:answer/id #uuid "8d705007-aa0c-5cde-a573-738e2aff9dca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385511} {:answer/id #uuid "03fdb318-277a-5b7b-bf02-538ad5ca1565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385512} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c5a255-ae95-5c0d-ae54-45b0dbd4189d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385513} {:answer/id #uuid "ab101cb8-1cc9-52ea-95e4-ec2e563d3dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{41}(5i-4j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385514}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385514} :answer-field/id #uuid "20d844bc-8ba7-554c-ae01-efce8b8ec0bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "522c74bc-3c1b-5bd8-a1b2-9e3f5a7b89a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-71383" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(5,4).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(5,4)$, can be found by computing $∇f(5,4).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(5,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\text{ }i-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(5,4) & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{(5)^{2}-(4)^{2}}}\\text{ }i-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{(5)^{2}-(4)^{2}}}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{9}}i-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{9}}j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}i-\\frac{4}{3}j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(5,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(5,4)}{‖∇f(5,4)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{3}i-\\frac{4}{3}j}{\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{3}i-\\frac{4}{3}j}{\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{41}(5i-4j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385515 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc8cbbb3-0607-5384-b0f5-a7494df17199" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385517} {:answer/id #uuid "5f547d94-e922-5fd4-abac-19d655c1fdb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385518} {:answer/id #uuid "89704038-a5bc-5557-9fe3-df6fbe0297f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385519} {:answer/id #uuid "cd40c970-36db-556d-9137-5a131afc6b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385520} {:answer/id #uuid "459e2e49-2c9a-525a-a910-70de65a071a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385521}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385520} :answer-field/id #uuid "845fb0f9-d411-5b77-8fc8-10943f6e52ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385516}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "50913094-4471-56fc-97f7-275492373a24" :question/math-academy-id "q-71386" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+{y}^{2}=1.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(3,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+{y}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(3,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(3,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(3,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}x\\,i+2y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,0) & =\\frac{2}{9}(3)\\,i+2(0)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\,i \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(3,0)=⟨\\frac{2}{3},0⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(3,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-{x}_{0}){v}_{y}-(y-{y}_{0}){v}_{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-3)0-(y-0)\\frac{2}{3} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}y & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385522 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5decc890-1d01-5ef2-9154-9ed30e0d6582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+2y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385524} {:answer/id #uuid "a0817702-700e-5e7c-8da2-a35776ec98ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+y+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385525} {:answer/id #uuid "569c2b8b-c5da-52e2-b41d-09fbec22be67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385526} {:answer/id #uuid "0109f1c6-5278-5400-a02b-474b0e4272e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-2y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385527} {:answer/id #uuid "92ed1ce6-3824-5dec-a381-53a6e97e1d99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-y+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385528}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385524} :answer-field/id #uuid "f683d60b-f987-566f-8e73-598501a40b7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4a6c1cae-376e-5dfa-b07c-5886b3b91c3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71389" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)={x}^{2}+y.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy) & ={x}^{2}+y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}-y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}-y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}-y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}-y)\\,j \\\\  & =(-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2x)\\,i+(-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-1)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,0) & =(-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡((-1)\\cdot 0)-2\\cdot (-1))\\,i+(-(-1)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡((-1)\\cdot 0)-1)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i-j \\\\  & =⟨2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-1,0)=⟨2,-1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(-1,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(-1)}{2} & =\\frac{y-0}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{2} & =-y \\\\ x+1 & =-2y \\\\ x+2y+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385529 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ac0e82c-e65c-52b4-92cf-ea9eef5506dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi \\text{ }i-\\pi \\text{ }j+\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385531} {:answer/id #uuid "725ac3d7-7213-5bdb-b47f-4954c2a469c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi \\text{ }i+\\pi \\text{ }j+\\pi \\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385532} {:answer/id #uuid "44d96ff8-6e7a-5e3c-bdfc-e4fa56a64251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi \\text{ }i+\\pi \\text{ }j+\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385533} {:answer/id #uuid "5a3bbb8f-e6f1-5641-8261-340795b58dda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{ }i-\\pi \\text{ }j+\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385534} {:answer/id #uuid "fa3e1f03-a9e4-5b31-b950-95579c95df2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\pi \\text{ }i+\\pi \\text{ }j+\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385535}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385531} :answer-field/id #uuid "7027997b-9ea2-56b2-bbd7-bdf567a4b72d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "aef7acb8-89a1-5016-aa4c-ee6f59af7efc" :question/math-academy-id "q-71426" :question/problem "Evaluate $∇h(x,y,z)$ at the point $(1,-1,\\pi )$ if $h(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz) \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =xz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz) \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHence, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇h(x,y,z) & ={h}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }i+{h}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{h}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\text{ }i+xz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\text{ }j+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇h(1,-1,\\pi ) & =(-1)(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )\\text{ }i+(1)(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )\\text{ }j+(1)(-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(-\\pi )(-1)\\text{ }i+\\pi (-1)\\text{ }j-(-1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\pi \\text{ }i-\\pi \\text{ }j+\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385536 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f709b8d-e22d-555e-8f82-4b819b741a2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385538} {:answer/id #uuid "4f628e18-1e56-5031-998e-e94575d669fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-2,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385539} {:answer/id #uuid "8a817cfe-6e50-5534-9c89-401a91beed6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(4,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385540} {:answer/id #uuid "6f89f99e-c222-5f75-b88f-1f3ed3be632f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(4,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385541} {:answer/id #uuid "55d2d586-a13f-55b7-99b9-71d0fd4b3d5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385542}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385538} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5955cf1-cc26-55ec-8c2a-34ff0cdd5429" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385537}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "191461eb-d37c-55af-a5cc-b1cdcbe5989a" :question/math-academy-id "q-71437" :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y)=4xy+{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}$, find the points $(x,y)$ where $∇g(x,y)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy+{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =4y+0-2x \\\\  & =2(2y-x) \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy+{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =4x+2y-0 \\\\  & =2(2x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇g(x,y)=2(2y-x)\\,i+2(2x+y)\\,j.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇g(x,y)=0:$\n\n$$\n2(2y-x)\\,i+2(2x+y)\\,j=0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2y-x &=0 \\\\ 2x+y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=2y.$ Substituting these into the second equation, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2y)+y & =0 \\\\ 5y & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get $x=2\\cdot 0=0.$\n\nTherefore, $∇g(x,y)=0$ at the point $(0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385543 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00852e0a-f6f6-5c5a-ac0e-5aab7917034a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385545} {:answer/id #uuid "0a138de7-1cdb-5fca-ab9b-192f03014909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(e,e,e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385546} {:answer/id #uuid "4db23065-4328-5f87-98fa-c2f53e71285d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{e},\\frac{1}{e},\\frac{1}{e})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385547} {:answer/id #uuid "c45fae02-a3c6-5e5b-83c3-515185ddf33b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,-1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385548} {:answer/id #uuid "965bb97e-ab1f-5bcd-b8b9-68650da44bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1,1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385549}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385549} :answer-field/id #uuid "12e34691-896f-5bb6-afac-57e259bb49f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ceb4f40b-ddfe-5165-8e9a-092d10842d64" :question/math-academy-id "q-71475" :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+z\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-x-y-z$, find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $∇g(x,y,z)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+z\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-x-y-z) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+0+0-1-0-0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+z\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-x-y-z) \\\\  & =0+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y\\cdot \\frac{1}{y}+0-0-1-0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+1-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+z\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-x-y-z) \\\\  & =0+0+\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+z\\cdot \\frac{1}{z}-0-0-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+1-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇g(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\,k.$ \n\nNow, we solve $∇g(x,y,z)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficient of $i$, $j$, and $k$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x &=0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y &=0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡z &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=1.$  Similarly, from the second and third equations, we obtain $y=1$ and $z=1.$\n\nHence, $∇g(x,y,z)=0$ at the point $(1,1,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385550 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be7f21f4-cabe-54eb-bc85-93d694a5ed38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x){e}^{xy}\\text{ }i+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2y){e}^{xy}\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385552} {:answer/id #uuid "4ec1d0e5-cc37-5321-849e-8e1b3950c06b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "({x}^{2}y+{y}^{3}){e}^{xy}\\text{ }i+({x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}){e}^{xy}\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385553} {:answer/id #uuid "3ad1fd13-781b-5d24-9468-b6d312c780db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "({x}^{2}y+{y}^{3}+2x){e}^{xy}\\text{ }i+({x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}+2y){e}^{xy}\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385554} {:answer/id #uuid "f82a086d-2718-5c14-b976-c8999ecd57bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "({x}^{2}y+{y}^{3}+2x)\\text{ }i+({x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}+2y)\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385555} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9d2f1c-dc91-5316-9ad5-65fd3a68e1cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x{e}^{xy}\\text{ }i+2y{e}^{xy}\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385556}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385554} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3b75486-11a8-5732-8e0c-87ceee70a576" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "48290c67-cc9b-570f-83c1-2b1af7db25a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-71484" :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $g(x,y)h(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x,y) & ={e}^{xy},\\, &  & ∇g(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}\\text{ }i+x{e}^{xy}\\text{ }j, &  &  \\\\ h(x,y) & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\, &  & ∇h(x,y)=2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $g(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[gh]=h\\,∇\\,g+g\\,∇\\,h\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[g(x,y)h(x,y)] & =h(x,y)∇g(x,y)+g(x,y)∇h(x,y) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})(y{e}^{xy}\\text{ }i+x{e}^{xy}\\text{ }j)+{e}^{xy}(2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =(({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})y{e}^{xy}\\text{ }i+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})x{e}^{xy}\\text{ }j)+(2x{e}^{xy}\\text{ }i+2y{e}^{xy}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}y+{y}^{3}+2x){e}^{xy}\\text{ }i+({x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}+2y){e}^{xy}\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385755 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "564d868a-9fde-5394-a0a7-ee34c0c4d724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385757} {:answer/id #uuid "a85952d3-86d1-5ecc-ad78-7dcdecf1e567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{u}^{2}-uv+{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385758} {:answer/id #uuid "ff785874-5a5d-5b25-b151-f49876d09835" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385759} {:answer/id #uuid "832628ab-7157-5368-b5e4-55126e572f4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4(u-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385760} {:answer/id #uuid "e3966b05-dcec-5f9b-b1c6-e7031cf3e401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385761}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385759} :answer-field/id #uuid "38071234-9bc0-5b00-b590-cccd8d7c31d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "defe61a0-21a4-5ecd-abfb-89db5317a74b" :question/math-academy-id "q-81264" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}-{v}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}.$  \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & -2v \\\\ 2u & 2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot 2v-(-2v)\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =4uv+4uv \\\\  & =8uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387215 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c793c767-8ecf-564e-af97-7c0e5166064b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1/3}^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+8}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387217} {:answer/id #uuid "71c2e31f-1ce7-5e4f-bb9e-8d3e5faf7e68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+1}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387218} {:answer/id #uuid "b13d0e88-37bf-5316-8237-40c1c7c1a505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{1/3}^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387219} {:answer/id #uuid "ae35c1a8-9ade-537f-b9ff-8d9b8d021811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387220} {:answer/id #uuid "e2a101a7-ad66-5ad3-8847-9c8591f60fb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+8}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387221}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387220} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4412e87-ff23-50d1-8bf7-efc4e936bf02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3bf8f8b4-ea1a-5394-a193-4b55a8b9d41a" :question/math-academy-id "q-122542" :question/problem "![](images/62/62f33dab4305a41a0bc180231fef7015361dec4aac63f14c31fd48a39b8fcd68.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\frac{2}{y}$ traversed between the points $(2,1)$ and $(\\frac{1}{3},6),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{2}{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{2}{t},\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 6\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 6$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2}{t}\\right)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{{t}^{4}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4+{t}^{4}}{{t}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=4x{y}^{2},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4\\left(\\frac{2}{t}\\right)(t)^{2} \\\\  & =8t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{6}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{6}8t\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387256 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fbd8e3fe-9258-5fd4-ac38-46ac64db6bc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,i-{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387258} {:answer/id #uuid "da8671ca-6c00-5ad9-a9e3-7c2197ed1890" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,i-2y{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387259} {:answer/id #uuid "e5372737-b3d4-5fdf-b4fd-fc53a2121ec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-2y)\\,i-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-2y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387260} {:answer/id #uuid "0bcb5dce-0d72-5149-b95d-b553c17f8c50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-2y)\\,i+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-2y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387261} {:answer/id #uuid "d4e00fb2-b549-5885-b25c-39af3314dd26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,i-2{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387262}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387262} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1c2ee55-5d94-5079-b34c-8536d3fd9a20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b9b6323d-ff08-50ce-be1a-fa1f61c66864" :question/math-academy-id "q-122608" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-2y).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-2y)) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-2y)) \\\\  & =-2{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,i-2{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387263 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c7d1d36-652e-5181-9302-abbb5df8798b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/14/1461b0737bf83fee8e16c1e5e102c36c2042e46935550ebc08a0d5774a561cd3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387265} {:answer/id #uuid "293009e5-bb46-5343-920e-66801c6cfbce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ac/acb2066428e0660e7d4e106acd5faf60600d6f93de0b6cf4c43ee2b5864e6e51.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387266} {:answer/id #uuid "7be00e9c-596e-5c63-ac00-60deba93c44a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/0d/0d5f1fb1b13459937f7bd3ff0ed8b6da641fcd346dc961d7ec5b98be29e45b1d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387267} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9fdbaf-e58e-56ff-981f-39b0969f23ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/cd/cdebaf3d8c07fe6cb26c4fb2c5b72d2bd60dc968b3345fc1c4a5737d8c6c2b53.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387268} {:answer/id #uuid "e227d23f-1359-5012-8c53-e4feec2f7c4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8a/8a35641fa57b01a95a0c1d5b3dba8a0072b291a6822ac7b2f5e0473b9037796b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387269}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387265} :answer-field/id #uuid "07a54f60-4401-5cf2-ba14-b1330ea47fc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "40689dc7-e924-59ea-9bdd-9f2acdedd9ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-122619" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=x+{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\,i+2y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-4,1),$ $(1,1),$ and $(3,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-4,1) & =i+2\\cdot 1\\,j=i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =i+2\\cdot 1\\,j=i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,-1) & =i+2\\cdot (-1)\\,j=i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/14/1461b0737bf83fee8e16c1e5e102c36c2042e46935550ebc08a0d5774a561cd3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387270 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bec373fa-27cc-5734-871a-ec77749ea6a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/0c/0c425b6fcb88f4c958a7c0371a64a815eda5ebf8ed14b0adc06875de1e24003b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387272} {:answer/id #uuid "6120694d-f5d6-5a30-a9b1-04836470b4fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/af/af7fdf48d36f477174867761b90d9349344029980012f1f7907f544efd3893bc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387273} {:answer/id #uuid "5738a2a6-58f2-5cb8-befa-f76d6d0fb2c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/48/48c57f24904442c74178682db34aaa0113eb84042c78516a4e6f961e73b0f9eb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387274} {:answer/id #uuid "11eee978-2cbc-57da-8a9a-9ffddf6422c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/02/020c3ec7cd0e5e57bb4c6de8516988187c9038d9e5598b116966b71fa8b917d8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387275} {:answer/id #uuid "04551b05-601c-557a-a5a2-69cba4463197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/25/25ed4b4750662433bf4405b5e63ab78396688a985c76e2d34d449c0689830380.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387276}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387275} :answer-field/id #uuid "c39f6b60-6db1-56ec-93e1-e634b438a208" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "14b7ceda-a408-53c9-aeb5-05da06c84fba" :question/math-academy-id "q-122732" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & ={y}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\,i+2xy\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,1),$ $(2,1),$ and $(-2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,1) & ={1}^{2}\\,i+2\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 1\\,j=i-4j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & ={1}^{2}\\,i+2\\cdot 2\\cdot 1\\,j=i+4\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-2,-1) & =(-1)^{2}\\,i+2\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (-1)\\,j=i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/02/020c3ec7cd0e5e57bb4c6de8516988187c9038d9e5598b116966b71fa8b917d8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387277 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de1513c2-3155-5def-a5b5-5b833a833652" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b6/b6a7c1faba2bb5d18d5f729d2576d7475f6ce1784698fae0a1c1ea97b80d6180.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387279} {:answer/id #uuid "f9bf0d9a-d009-5dbb-8f91-ece881c3e02c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d4/d44395fa166c654c41322bd31e1a9bf9bc6c7a398e27da7f9de9d249ccdb1107.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387280} {:answer/id #uuid "0b6544d5-cd56-547b-895e-df2376403680" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8c/8cdc92839252560ad52b54bb77d9db75ba066e33bfa5a3c451e4cbb6345e9d33.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387281} {:answer/id #uuid "8bade9cb-3b17-5e2f-ab51-5cbf5f1198f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/25/2560beb4dcff267890c47ad5df8ac7aef0e5de64a57384f8c71e35d8325c892b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387282} {:answer/id #uuid "45a0eeab-6f8a-5b04-8150-613eb8858a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8e/8ec9af02a374c1b8a954117ad055f78e9e2ac74bc391bb230968198a3a474fef.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387283}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387283} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc3d49d5-a5f3-575b-83f9-afd438ed4ffd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7e446a55-b0d3-59f4-abde-52af19baebe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-122745" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=\\frac{y}{xy+1}\\,i+\\frac{x}{xy+1}\\,j\n$$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,0),(1,-2),$ and $(0,2).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=\\frac{y}{xy+1}\\,i+\\frac{x}{xy+1}\\,j\\,:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-2,0) & =\\frac{0}{(-2)(0)+1}\\,i+\\frac{-2}{(-2)(0)+1}\\,j=-2\\,j \\\\ F(1,-2) & =\\frac{-2}{(1)(-2)+1}\\,i+\\frac{1}{(1)(-2)+1}\\,j=2\\,i-j \\\\ F(0,2) & =\\frac{2}{(0)(2)+1}\\,i+\\frac{0}{(0)(2)+1}\\,j=2\\,i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/8e/8ec9af02a374c1b8a954117ad055f78e9e2ac74bc391bb230968198a3a474fef.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387284 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9a9e026-4bc7-575e-be23-f281b7660b3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d6/d688cdc804635688df6780bfb674622a491528c85802397cc3dccb6bd4a2d6be.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387286} {:answer/id #uuid "60880678-2b0d-57f7-8c8d-ae95a3c4b0f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/1f/1f597a7886abe6a8cddca9192658c8ded57dd96b010dfbf166e8cea6484872e7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387287} {:answer/id #uuid "71e0ba0c-ade1-5e87-9ac1-d66c6f763265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/11/114f65bec341c3bef23ffda186b7d5a721e89e607d15c64624b6145e734c68c0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387288} {:answer/id #uuid "4150b677-4ab5-5642-97f3-e462d77d1b78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8b/8b20800589467fa460547519312ed283d373ce2b7553b0c397413799b2d6597f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387289} {:answer/id #uuid "bcafb174-7327-5bc3-a3a6-55a1c3530cdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/53/5317b575c101e4a782e17f9f83f56f66f8151a1d1c12cd0084020429435ba542.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387290}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387286} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb7e4ea8-2187-5984-93cd-b8162a3b3097" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "709ee56e-badf-53e5-8fe9-cd6e97119ca1" :question/math-academy-id "q-122752" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=(y-2x)\\,i+(2y-4x)\\,j$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(1,3),(-1,-1),$ and $(3,2).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=(y-2x)\\,i+(2y-4x)\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1,3) & =(3-2(1))\\,i+(2(3)-4(1))\\,j=i+2\\,j \\\\ F(-1,-1) & =(-1-2(-1))\\,i+(2(-1)-4(-1))\\,j=i+2\\,j \\\\ F(3,2) & =(2-2(3))\\,i+(2(2)-4(3))\\,j=-4\\,i-8\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/d6/d688cdc804635688df6780bfb674622a491528c85802397cc3dccb6bd4a2d6be.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387291 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d40339cb-9f8c-56b1-9446-2bb79c2ae322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/4c/4c9fc6ae3cbbf6fa3d241d3607b26fee1cd0670fde37494638ca90da2b02ecfa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387293} {:answer/id #uuid "3b36441e-f03d-5f2c-b78a-49b57116a3cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d6/d6084f0711f728a09a78c9803bec9c6e54274d6d72a2adc2b1547651e0ec702c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387294} {:answer/id #uuid "116d6c88-d5ff-5163-b1bc-a148e6194baf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/3e/3e9efea8998b1dbaab3cc6d89dce4f99bdb06066e81fcb78a2b3a0e412e2e44c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387295} {:answer/id #uuid "e73588d4-0678-5e1c-aac4-ffad61b46f23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/80/80ecb3a560bc23359c3b44baace0523bfe653f0c5a5fee5b5c646d901ade5feb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387296} {:answer/id #uuid "d1c0c612-25c2-5765-8c88-fa1c6a482813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/04/040d62d956945b7bb67cae560cda4e09a22be2fa9cd2e5da3f3cc372269f14e4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387297}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387294} :answer-field/id #uuid "c92e3f54-7df7-528f-9e7f-fbd995c31ee0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5b5b3650-421e-53e6-b6b4-9b4c7a243072" :question/math-academy-id "q-122754" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=(y-2)\\,i+(x+2)\\,j$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-5,3),(0,0),$ and $(2,-3).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=(y-2)\\,i+(x+2)\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-5,3) & =(3-2)\\,i+(-5+2)\\,j=i-3\\,j \\\\ F(0,0) & =(0-2)\\,i+(0+2)\\,j=-2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ F(2,-3) & =(-3-2)\\,i+(2+2)\\,j=-5\\,i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/d6/d6084f0711f728a09a78c9803bec9c6e54274d6d72a2adc2b1547651e0ec702c.png)"}]}
